urn some of his own paintings that were  considered heretical in the renaissance  things changed a bit painters like  leonardo da vinci defined their artistic  duty as uniting aesthetics and morality  into beautiful moral art but the  enlightenment changed everything the  free thinking more secular era saw  european artists largely emancipated  from moral policing what followed as  scholar arnold berlant writes was an  aberration in arts history as artists  finally became basically autonomous of  course this freedom left some  disgruntled and over the centuries has  led to artists like d.h lawrence being  taken to court over their arts supposed  immorality though his publisher penguin  books was the one on trial and we can  only hope mr penguin was forced to take  the stand critiques of a moral art have  continued into the modern day typically  surfacing as moral panics over the art  children are consuming rock and roll was  going to turn the teens into sex  monsters in the 50s and the 80s and 90s  saw major hysteria about violence in rap  video games action movies and so on  surely kids were being prepped for lives  of crime of course crime rates plummeted  straight up plummeted in the 90s but  while that facts get in the way of a  good old-fashioned moral panic at the  same time there's been a recent  reckoning with some of history's more  questionable works of art take birth of  a nation which was once hailed as an  astonishing feat of early american  filmmaking today it is better known for  almost single-handedly reviving the kkk  and today the tension between art and  morality lingers artists aren't being  explicitly policed by say the church at  the same time they are subject to an  increasingly loud court of public  opinion that especially on social media  is often quite critical of art's  perceived moral shortcomings take the  loki hysterics surrounding the release  of the hunt which reimagined america's  political divide as hide and seek but  had shotguns with all this we think the  real overarching question when it comes  to ethical criticism of art is does the  implied ethics of a work of art affect  its aesthetic value scholar noel carroll  characterized the two main opposing  sides in the debate as such on one side  there's autonomism this is the view that  as carroll writes art and ethics are  autonomous realms of value and thus  criteria from the ethical realm should  not be imported to evaluate the  aesthetic realm this is also called  aestheticism and its inherents argue  that art should be considered valuable  for its aesthetic qualities not for its  ability to morally enlighten or improve  us as proponent oscar wilde put it there  is no such thing as a moral or an  immoral book books are well written or  badly written that is all more commonly  moderate autonomism holds that it's  possible to morally evaluate art but  it's just not relevant to whether that  art is actually good or bad  aesthetically speaking on the other side  there's moralism this is the view that  if a work of art has immoral  implications those implications  contribute to its aesthetic defects  therefore the aesthetic value of art can  be compromised by its immorality now  let's take a closer look at each side  autonomism has quite a philosophical  history the likes of human kant believe  that we have to separate aesthetic and  moral faculties philosopher francis  hutchison considered those faculties  distinctive enough to be considered like  different senses as in hearing versus  seeing just like you can't use your ears  to evaluate the crumb on this week's  bake off you can't use your moral  compass to evaluate art kant believed  that what we consider pleasurable  aesthetically is our taste and what we  consider pleasurable morally is simply  our moral feeling in more recent years  scholar clive bell has argued that art  or at least visual art needs to satisfy  two essential features it provokes a  particular sort of emotional response 