ions are  the ones valued by tolstoy's humanist  brand of christianity deep religious  feeling horror and injustice and so on  moral virtue and artistic virtue become  one and the same some moralists point to  art that is  pretty objectively morally troubling to  support their argument triumph of the  will is a common example made by the  nazi state's favorite director lenny  riefenstahl the movie unabashedly  worships hitler and his ideology it is  at the same time an aesthetically  creative and groundbreaking work of  cinema but more or less argue that any  aesthetic achievements are sullied by  the film's objectively deep world view  now nobody wants to be the guy who  argues in favor of triumph of the will  but at first blush  we do feel like the autonomous make a  good point after all didactic art with a  moral worldview can have little  aesthetic value take something like the  blindside which takes the morally  correct stance on the question of should  we be racist while at the same time  offering very little in the realm of  aesthetics what's more lots of good  abstract art has plenty of aesthetic  value but has no objective moral value  carol calls the argument regarding  abstract art the common denominator  argument and he finds it the most  persuasive of the autonomous basically  we must be able to apply our criterion  for evaluating art to all kinds of art  but as he explains since it is the case  that much art does not concern morality  directly ethics cannot be the  appropriate criterion for evaluating art  if you can't apply moral judgment to  every piece of art that is why should  you apply it to any that said we see the  moralist point when it comes to things  like triumph of the will or birth of the  nation so we're left pretty torn but  before you despair there's one viewpoint  that offers a life-saving curveball and  was given by writer and philosopher iris  murdoch for murdock the function of the  best art is to pierce the veil and let  us see things as they truly are  but for her this vision is inseparable  from virtue and to convey a truthful  vision of the world the artist needs to  understand human virtue truth then  becomes the real morality murdoch  believed that the best art holds up a  mirror to reality including and in  particular moral reality but if an  artist wants us to better understand  reality and morality they need to  understand it in the first place under  this thinking triumph of the will and  birth of the nation can't possibly be  considered great works of art because  they don't show a true picture of the  world they're both functionally racist  fever dreams philosopher ah hannah takes  it one step further arguing that art can  even push a society's morality forward  art is then seen throughout history to  be continually helping in the creation  of new values in the trans valuation of  values like how get out can make us  think about the way white liberals  contribute to systematic racism or how  parasite can make us rethink inequality  or how first reformed reframes the  environmental justice movement to ask  should a drunk horny priest blow up his  church now without committing to  moralism let's entertain the notion that  the ethical content of a work is  directly relevant to its value as art if  that's true it's natural to ask whether  a piece of art could help us see the  world more clearly by openly depicting  immoral content some widely acclaimed  films about war and organized crime  might fit the bill or 90s rap that gave  us an unflinching picture of what life  was like in places like queens or  compton it's hard to imagine how  illmatic could have been improved by a  cleaner more unproblematic moral stance  or that the godfather would have been  better art if nobody had played pranks  with horse heads  philosopher jon stewart mill said that  he who knows only his own side of the  case knows little of that his reasons  may be good and no one may have been  able to refute them 