ith all this we think the  real overarching question when it comes  to ethical criticism of art is does the  implied ethics of a work of art affect  its aesthetic value scholar noel carroll  characterized the two main opposing  sides in the debate as such on one side  there's autonomism this is the view that  as carroll writes art and ethics are  autonomous realms of value and thus  criteria from the ethical realm should  not be imported to evaluate the  aesthetic realm this is also called  aestheticism and its inherents argue  that art should be considered valuable  for its aesthetic qualities not for its  ability to morally enlighten or improve  us as proponent oscar wilde put it there  is no such thing as a moral or an  immoral book books are well written or  badly written that is all more commonly  moderate autonomism holds that it's  possible to morally evaluate art but  it's just not relevant to whether that  art is actually good or bad  aesthetically speaking on the other side  there's moralism this is the view that  if a work of art has immoral  implications those implications  contribute to its aesthetic defects  therefore the aesthetic value of art can  be compromised by its immorality now  let's take a closer look at each side  autonomism has quite a philosophical  history the likes of human kant believe  that we have to separate aesthetic and  moral faculties philosopher francis  hutchison considered those faculties  distinctive enough to be considered like  different senses as in hearing versus  seeing just like you can't use your ears  to evaluate the crumb on this week's  bake off you can't use your moral  compass to evaluate art kant believed  that what we consider pleasurable  aesthetically is our taste and what we  consider pleasurable morally is simply  our moral feeling in more recent years  scholar clive bell has argued that art  or at least visual art needs to satisfy  two essential features it provokes a  particular sort of emotional response 